 "notes": "Curve traced from vector paths of Fig. 3 (bottom/right panel, file constraints_zoom_3sigma.pdf in the e-print): the opaque dark-blue 'This work' line, i.e. the outermost (99% CL / '3sigma' per Sec. IV: 'strongest 3sigma bound to date on the effective ALP-photon coupling on the 8-25 eV mass range') upper bound on g_agamma from gamma-ray optical-depth measurements. Includes the dashed high-mass continuation (log10 m/eV > ~1.31, masses affected by neutral-hydrogen absorption), clipped to the plotted range -0.2 <= log10(m c^2/eV) <= 1.6 (0.63-40 eV). The 68% and 95% contours are closed credible regions (a 2.1 sigma preference for an ALP signal exists), so the exclusion curve is the 99% boundary; the paper explicitly prefers to interpret the result as an upper bound. Axis calibration from tick-label positions; g = 10^logg GeV^-1, m = 10^logm eV. Bound assumes ALPs are all of the (cosmological) dark matter; it depends on the mean cosmic DM abundance, not the local halo density, so no rho_DM rescaling applied.",
